-- examples/checks/retract-again.rofl — A DELTA ASKED FOR AND THE WORLD EVALUATED AGAIN, WRITTEN IN THE WORLD. The
-- world retracts ra_gone(1) (the check's retract option), the only fact of a relation a rule reads the edb of, so
-- the Rust engine evaluates again; it writes evaluated_again(Request, Reason) in the kernel's book, and a rule reads
-- it. Rust only: the TypeScript engine evaluates every change again and writes no row.
ra_name(ra_gone).
ra_gone(1).
ra_lone(R) :- ra_name(R), not edb(R).
ra_again(Why) :- evaluated_again(retract(_), Why).
-- expect-row: ra_lone[main](ra_gone)
-- expect-row: evaluated_again[$kernel](retract($fact(ra_gone,main,$cons(1,$nil))),"the retraction takes an edb or authority row a rule reads")
-- expect-row: ra_again[main]("the retraction takes an edb or authority row a rule reads")
